artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the production of the charmonium state ψ′ (the radial excitation of the J/ψ) in high-energy nuclear collisions at the CERN SPS. Define the ψ′ nuclear modification ratio as the ψ′ yield in nucleus-nucleus collisions divided by the yield expected from a Glauber-type extrapolation of the systematics measured in proton-nucleus (p-A) collisions, where ordinary nuclear absorption of the pre-resonant cc̄ state is parameterized by an absorption cross section fitted to p-A data. Experimentally, in sulphur-uranium (S-U) and lead-lead (Pb-Pb) collisions at SPS energies, the ψ′ yield exhibits a suppression significantly stronger than this p-A baseline extrapolation, i.e. an "extra" suppression beyond ordinary cold nuclear matter effects.

Determine the physical origin of this additional ψ′ suppression in S-U and Pb-Pb collisions relative to the p-A baseline. In particular, decide which of the following mechanisms (or which combination thereof) is responsible:
(a) interactions of the ψ′ (or its precursor cc̄ state) with ordinary hadronic/confined matter produced in the collision (so-called comover or hadronic absorption);
(b) dissociation ("melting") of the loosely bound ψ′ charmonium state in a deconfined quark-gluon plasma formed in the collision;
(c) the onset of a critical energy density already in the most peripheral S-U or Pb-Pb collisions at SPS energies, producing the same dissociation mechanism even at low centrality;
(d) some alternative mechanism not covered by (a)–(c).

Provide a quantitative model that reproduces the centrality dependence of the ψ′ suppression in both S-U and Pb-Pb at SPS, is consistent with the absence of such extra suppression in p-A, and distinguishes among the above scenarios.

